WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.460
Where does the nitrogen in a cut forest's stream come from?

2
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:12.300
Cut a forest, keep it from growing back, and its stream carries far more nitrogen

3
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.300
than before.

4
00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:17.730
Nobody added fertiliser, and the logs stayed where they fell.

5
00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.930
To find the source, keep accounts, the way a bank does.

6
00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:24.970
First, draw a line around the valley.

7
00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:30.933
Inside the line, nitrogen sits in pools, such as the soil and the trees.

8
00:00:32.293 --> 00:00:33.893
A pool is like a bank balance.

9
00:00:35.073 --> 00:00:38.273
A flux is a transfer, like a deposit or a withdrawal.

10
00:00:39.383 --> 00:00:42.283
Here nitrogen crosses the line in two ways.

11
00:00:43.503 --> 00:00:47.983
From the air, rain and snow deposit 8 kg per hectare each year.

12
00:00:49.343 --> 00:00:50.483
Microbes fix 6 more.

13
00:00:51.483 --> 00:00:53.003
It leaves in two ways.

14
00:00:54.143 --> 00:00:58.323
The stream carries 3 away, and microbes release 2 to the air as gas.

15
00:00:59.417 --> 00:01:02.257
The store changes only by what crosses the line.

16
00:01:03.857 --> 00:01:06.297
Change in stock = inputs − outputs.

17
00:01:07.597 --> 00:01:11.757
This forest gains 9 kg of nitrogen per hectare each year.

18
00:01:12.897 --> 00:01:16.237
The line and the year must be stated before any adding.

19
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:20.680
But most nitrogen never crosses the line at all.

20
00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.220
Decomposers release it from dead leaves as ammonium.

21
00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:29.120
Trees take it up, grow leaves, and drop them again.

22
00:01:30.183 --> 00:01:33.963
That loop moves about 90 kg per hectare each year.

23
00:01:35.143 --> 00:01:39.543
Here it is the biggest flux in the valley, far bigger than all the inputs together.

24
00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:42.847
Yet the counter never sees it.

25
00:01:43.967 --> 00:01:48.487
Nitrogen leaving one pool in the loop enters another pool inside the same line.

26
00:01:49.767 --> 00:01:51.667
In the sum, those transfers cancel.

27
00:01:53.127 --> 00:01:54.327
Cancelling is bookkeeping.

28
00:01:55.667 --> 00:01:57.107
It does not mean the loop is small.

29
00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:01.210
Now cut the trees, and keep new plants from growing.

30
00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:04.930
Does the loop simply stop, or does it go somewhere?

31
00:02:11.517 --> 00:02:12.697
It goes somewhere.

32
00:02:14.177 --> 00:02:17.817
Decomposers keep releasing nitrogen, but no roots take it up.

33
00:02:19.297 --> 00:02:23.417
Microbes turn the extra ammonium into nitrate, which soil holds weakly.

34
00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.360
So the loop breaks open and spills across the line.

35
00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:32.020
Nitrate pours out in the stream, and the counter turns negative.

36
00:02:33.233 --> 00:02:37.593
Likens, Bormann and colleagues saw this at Hubbard Brook, in New Hampshire.

37
00:02:38.893 --> 00:02:43.653
The stream from their cut watershed carried far more nitrate and calcium than the

38
00:02:43.653 --> 00:02:44.653
uncut one.

39
00:02:45.333 --> 00:02:47.873
Rain brought in no more nitrogen than before.

40
00:02:49.153 --> 00:02:51.713
The forest's own recycled nitrogen was leaving.

41
00:02:53.013 --> 00:02:57.373
A store changes only across its boundary, and here the broken loop crossed it.
